  "holdout_metric": null,
  "surprise": "Crash, not the idea's fault: metric 5 (0, 0, 16) because pretraining ran without gradients to the block matrices. run() computes the plain network's state under torch.no_grad first in the autocast region; autocast caches the bf16 casts of the weights that pass makes, those casts carry no grad_fn, and the expanded pass with gradients reused them, so on every batch with thoughts qkv/proj/fc1/fc2 got no gradient (and Muon's CUDA graph, captured on step 1, kept replaying stale momentum). Reproduced under the pod's torch 2.9.1 on CPU: a toy Linear after such a pass raises 'does not require grad', and the real model's block matrices get zero gradient; torch 2.14 no longer caches casts made without grad, which is why the local check_model/CPU tests passed. Pretraining val loss 0.56-0.57 at 0 thoughts against 704's 0.085, held-out greedy 0.000, feedback_gain 0.019, train loss flat near 0.55-0.60 from step 500; 0 proofs in RL on seeds 0 and 1, seed 2 found 79 targets in 5 rounds and scored 16. The fix is one line after the no_grad pass, torch.clear_autocast_cache(): with it the block matrices get gradients under 2.9.1 and bench_pretrain.py --secs 60 gives val loss 0.0868 at 469 steps (704's bench: 0.0919 at 552), 10.4 steps/s steady against 704's 12.2 (the broken file's 12.0 was fast because the backward skipped the weight gradients), 16.8 GB peak, 95 samples/s at 8 thoughts with the Lean gate. A committed registration is immutable, so run_idea.py refused the fixed file under 705; the next session should register 706 as this patch plus that line. The fixed file, its diff and its registration text (same hypothesis, corrected speed numbers) are in artifacts/autoresearch_thoughts/705-fable_plain-state-feedback/pretrain_fixed.py, pretrain_fixed.patch and params_fixed.json (local, gitignored). One more correction for 706: the gain-1 bench (loss 0.74 at 543 steps) that motivated the zero gain init had the same bug, so that reading was wrong and the zero init is untested; 702's unit gain may be the better start.",
